126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Beauty has never been a static concept. It traverses the centuries, changing its face, carried by the sensibilities, beliefs, and dreams unique to each era. Far from being a universal and permanent truth, it is shaped to the rhythm of human societies, revealing their priorities as much as their collective imagination.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (In Antiquity, beauty was rooted in harmony and proportion. The ideal body reflected an almost philosophical balance among strength, proportion, and perfection. Aesthetics thus embodied a worldly order: being beautiful meant reflecting a certain idea of universal harmony. The gaze directed at the body transcended mere appearance to join a quest for the ideal.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (The Middle Ages profoundly alters this perception. Discretion and restraint become central values. Beauty becomes more inward, associated with moral purity and modesty. The body ceases to be celebrated for itself; it becomes the vessel for spiritual elevation. Elegance lies more in sobriety than in radiance.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (With the Renaissance, the body regains its place at the heart of aesthetics. Full, radiant figures symbolize vitality, fertility, and abundance. Feminine beauty then expresses prosperity and a newfound confidence in the earthly realm. Art celebrates the flesh with gentleness                                   .)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E ( and admiration, revealing a more humanistic view of appearance.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (The following centuries accelerate these transformations. The rise of fashion, photography, and eventually cinema introduces models visible on a massive scale. Beauty becomes a widely broadcast, reproduced, and sometimes standardized image. Public icons influence collective tastes, while aesthetic standards are renewed more rapidly than ever.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (In the contemporary era, social media multiplies these shifts even further. Trends circulate instantaneously, blending diverse cultural inspirations and personal styles. At the same time, a new sensibility emerges: that of a pluralistic beauty, less confined to a single ideal. Individuality, authenticity, and self-expression progressively take precedence over strict conformity.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Beauty standards change, therefore, because they accompany social, economic, and cultural transformations. They reflect the dominant values of a given moment, whether it be spirituality, abundance, modernity, or self-affirmation. Beauty thus appears as a collective creation, shaped as much by the human gaze as by time. It remains a living idea, forever in motion, a discreet yet faithful reflection of the history of mentalities and sensibilities.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
