524 0 obj
<<
/Title (In the 1960s, as the space race was in full swing, three African-American women, Katherine Johnson, Dorothy Vaughan, and Mary Jackson, worked in the shadows of NASA. Brilliant and determined, they had to overcome segregation and discrimination to contribute their essential skills: Katherine calculated crucial trajectories for space missions, Dorothy learned to program the first computers to anticipate calculations, and Mary fought to become an engineer despite legal and social obstacles. Their quiet but indispensable work enabled John Glenn and other astronauts to succeed in their missions, while breaking historic barriers for women in previously restricted             .)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 368 0 R
/Next 650 0 R
>>
